          questions: [
            { id: 'p1', label: '¶ 1', prompt: 'Why people obey authority', scriptures: ['Matt 23:5', 'Isa 48:17, 18'], c: {
              s: ['*Some people obey so they will not get in trouble.', 'Some people obey only when someone is watching.', 'Jehovah’s rules help us.'],
              h: ['*Some people obey only so they will not be punished or so others will praise them, Matthew 23:5.', 'Isaiah 48:17 says Jehovah teaches us to benefit ourselves.', 'We can see that obeying Jehovah is good for us.'],
              x: ['*King Joash did what was right all the days of Jehoiada the priest, 2 Chronicles 24:2. After Jehoiada died, Joash turned to idols. His obedience depended on someone watching over him. Jehovah wants obedience that lasts when no one is supervising.', 'Jesus said the religious leaders did their deeds to be seen by men, Matthew 23:5, even enlarging their scripture boxes. Obedience for show earns human praise but not God’s approval. Jesus said such people already have their reward in full, Matthew 6:2.', 'Isaiah 48:17, 18 shows Jehovah teaching us to benefit ourselves, with peace flowing like a river for those who listen. Seeing those benefits is a good reason to obey. Still, benefits alone may not hold us steady when obedience costs us something.']
            }},
            { id: 'p2', label: '¶ 2', prompt: 'Our main reason for obeying Jehovah', scriptures: ['Matt 22:37, 38', 'Rom 6:17'], c: {
              s: ['*We obey Jehovah because we love him.', 'We obey even when no one sees us.', 'Loving Jehovah makes us want to obey.'],
              h: ['*Jesus said the greatest commandment is to love Jehovah with our whole heart, Matthew 22:37, 38.', 'Romans 6:17 talks about obeying from the heart.', 'If we love Jehovah, we obey him even when nobody is watching.'],
              x: ['*Jesus said at John 14:31 that he did as the Father commanded so the world would know he loves the Father. For Jesus, obedience was an expression of love. When love is our motive, obedience becomes a gift to Jehovah, not a burden.', 'Joseph was alone with Potiphar’s wife with no one watching, yet he refused, asking how he could sin against God, Genesis 39:9. His love for Jehovah kept him obedient when he could have hidden the wrong. That is obedience from the heart.', 'Romans 6:17 commends those who became obedient from the heart. The heart includes our motives and desires. When we feed our love for Jehovah through personal study and prayer, obedience grows naturally rather than being forced.']
            }}
          ]
